# New lesson content rules

These rules apply every time a new lesson JSON is created (or an existing lesson gains new words, sentences or grammar).
Before writing the file, read every earlier lesson of the same book (the order in `content/index.json`) and collect:
their words (vocabulary, dictation words, glossary), their sentences (exercise answers and `lesson_content.sentences`),
their grammar rules and examples, and their sentence structures.

## 1. Words

- A new lesson must not repeat a word that an earlier lesson of the same book already teaches, in its word list or in
  its word exercises (vocabulary, dictation, synonyms and antonyms), not even in another form of the same base word
  (for example, if `eat` is taught, `eats` is a repeat in a later lesson). Phrases count too (`first name` is a repeat
  if an earlier lesson has it). Sentences, dialogues, stories and grammar examples may use such a word when a sentence
  needs it (for example `I`, `you`, `am`, `are`, `from`).
- Do not add words on your own. The new words are the lesson's planned words only.
- Every sentence, example and exercise may use only words that the learner already knows: words from earlier lessons,
  plus this lesson's own new words. If a sentence needs another word, that word must already be in an earlier lesson.
- No person names in the word list or in the word exercises (vocabulary, dictation): no first names, family names or
  nicknames (for example Ali, Habibi, Bahar, Amir). Names may appear in sentences, dialogues, stories and grammar
  examples, because a sentence about a person needs the name to make sense.
- Do not add a list of people (for example a `profiles` part in `lesson_content` with names, roles and cities). Such a
  list only exists to quiz the learner on people from the book, which the rule above forbids.
- Every vocabulary word has its pronunciation (IPA between slashes, like `/ˈɡɪvən/`) and its Persian meaning.
- Synonyms and antonyms: for every word of the lesson, find its synonyms and antonyms among the words of this lesson
  and of the earlier lessons of the same book. Each one found is written down as a `Synonym: <word>` or
  `Opposite: <word>` vocabulary exercise item, so the study page shows it. An earlier lesson's word that is a synonym
  or antonym of a new word is added to the new word this way, too.

### Word categories (groups of words)

- A word that belongs to a topic group gets a `category` field in its vocabulary entry, with the group name in English
 (for example `"category": "Countries"`). Reuse the exact name of an existing group. Only the groups the author asks
 for are used; do not invent groups for other words. A word with no group has no `category` field.
- A group is about one topic. For lesson 1.1, the Countries group holds every country, capital city and nationality
 of the country chart, plus the general words `country`, `capital city`, `nationality` and `nationalities`. Place names
 that are not countries or capitals (for example `New York`) are not in that group.
- The vocabulary array keeps each group in one block, in the order the group needs: for the Countries group, `capital city`,
 `country`, `nationality`, `nationalities`, then each country as capital city, country, nationality. The words list
 shows the groups first (in the order they first appear in the lesson), each group in this order, and the words
 without a group after them, A to Z.
- The words page shows one chip per group under the search box, with `All` first. The chips filter the list, Play and
 the card view. A lesson with no `category` on any word shows no chips.
- A group's English words must match the `vocabulary` words exactly, so the chart and the list use the same spelling.
- When an existing lesson gets or changes a group, bump its `version` in `content/index.json` and run `npm test`.
 `tests/lesson-vocabulary.test.js` checks the lesson 1.1 Countries block; add the same kind of check for any new group.

### Practice groups (the Practice page)

- Every practice item (any exercise type) that uses a word of a group gets a `group` field with the group's name, the
 same name as its vocabulary `category` (for example `"group": "Countries"`). It is `group` and not `category`, because
 `category` on a practice item is its exercise type. An item with no group has no `group` field.
- The Practice page shows one chip per group under "Group" (with `All` first), and the mission only asks the questions of
 the chosen group. The group must cover every word of the group: a country word with no practice item is a gap.
- `tests/lesson-practice-groups.test.js` checks the lesson 1.1 Countries group; add the same kind of check for any new group.

## 2. Sentences and grammar

- A sentence structure is taught once per book. Do not repeat a structure from an earlier lesson with different words.
  Example: if lesson 1 has `I am from Iran.`, lesson 2 must not add `I am from Germany.` The same rule applies to grammar
  examples and to grammar rules.
- Every sentence must be meaningful on its own. Sentences that belong together (a question and its answer, a short
  exchange) stay together as one exercise item, in order, so the learner can read the whole exchange in one place.
  Do not split a conversation across several items.
- Do not add an extra sentence to help a grammar point unless a sentence with that structure already exists in an
  earlier lesson of the same book.

## 3. Grammar section

- Every grammar example is a sentence of the lesson (the tests check this).
- English and Persian are always shown together. The grammar page has no English-only or Persian-only switch.
- Each grammar rule has a Persian explanation that points the learner to the goal of the rule.

## 4. Practice (exercises)

- Every new word of the lesson appears in the practice exercises.
- Practice sentences follow the grammar of the lesson and the sentences the learner has seen in it.
- Every answer that can be shortened has all of its forms in `accepted_answers`, so the learner can answer in any
  correct form. The app compares the text exactly, so a form that is not listed is marked wrong.
  - Pronoun + be: `I am` = `I'm`; `you are`, `we are`, `they are` = `you're`, `we're`, `they're`; `he is`, `she is`,
    `it is`, `that is` = `he's`, `she's`, `it's`, `that's`. (`this's` is not a form: keep `This is`.)
  - Negative: `is not` = `isn't`, `are not` = `aren't`, `was not` = `wasn't`, `were not` = `weren't`, `do not` =
    `don't`, `does not` = `doesn't`, `did not` = `didn't`, `will not` = `won't`, `have not` = `haven't`,
    `has not` = `hasn't`, `cannot` = `can't`.
  - Several parts can be shortened: list every combination. `No, I'm not. I'm from Medellín.` has four forms:
    `No, I am not. I am from Medellín.`, `No, I'm not. I am from Medellín.`, `No, I am not. I'm from Medellín.`,
    `No, I'm not. I'm from Medellín.`
  - A short form is not used at the end of a sentence where it is not allowed: `Yes, I am.` is not `Yes, I'm.`
  - Exceptions: a sentence builder uses the words of one form only (its word bank must match `expected_answer`), and a
    multiple choice option is one form only (an alternative form must not be a second option, because it would be a
    second correct answer).
- Include a few examples of every exercise type the game has, where the lesson's grammar allows it (translation, voice
  translation, word translation, sentence builder, grammar correction, negative, question, multiple choice, dictation,
  vocabulary, verb forms). The negative and question types show one example per sentence structure, not many words
  in the same structure.
- No person names in practice: a practice item (any exercise type) never asks the learner to pick, type or find a
 person's name, never lists people as options, and never asks a question whose answer depends on knowing a person
 from the book (for example "Which student is from New York?", "What was the name of the Spanish teacher?", or
 "Who is a teacher from Chicago?" with names as options). The learner has only seen the app's lessons, not the book,
 so such a question is not fair and does not teach anything. Remove such items instead of rewording them.
- A person's name may still appear in a practice sentence only as a plain introduction, where the grammar is the
 point (for example "Hi, Max. Are you a teacher?" or "My name is Karina"). The name must not be the answer, a
 choice, or the subject of the question.
- Every practice item has a hint (راهنما). A hint is a short guide that points to the rule or to the key word of that
  item. The same word must not be used to mean several different things in the hints.
- **Hints must not give the answer.** Under no circumstances may a hint spell out the English answer, reconstruct it
  step by step, or list the English chunks of the sentence in order (for example «first write What’s, then the name of
  …»). Do not copy half of `expected_answer` into the hint. If English appears at all, it may only be one or two
  **vocabulary** words the item is about (for example `college = کالج`), never phrases from the answer sentence.
- The hint may name the **grammar rule** or **sentence type** in Persian (سؤال، تأیید، منفی، …), point to an **earlier
  pattern** the learner already saw («مثل سؤال spell در Before starting»), or give the Persian meaning of a **new or
  tricky word** in that item. Keep it general: one small clue, not a recipe to type the line.
- Dictation and vocabulary items that ask for a single word or phrase may still use `word = meaning` style hints; that
  is not the same as a full-sentence translation item.

## 5. Files and checks

- Add the lesson to `content/index.json`. When an existing lesson changes, bump its `version` there.
- Lesson titles must follow the existing naming of the book.
- Run `npm test` before finishing. A test that fails because of these rules is a real problem to fix, not a number to
  change.
